# Orbit local development environment.
#
# Copy to .env and adjust as needed:  cp .env.example .env
# .env is gitignored. This file must never contain real secrets.

NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL
#
# DATABASE_URL is the single source of the database connection. Drizzle config,
# migrations, repositories, integration tests, and seed scripts read this one
# variable and nothing else — there are deliberately no separate
# POSTGRES_HOST/PORT/USER/PASSWORD parts that could drift out of sync with it.
#
# Local development targets a dedicated role and database on the PostgreSQL
# server already installed on this machine; see README > Local database.
# The password is a throwaway local development credential, not a secret.
#
# docker-compose.yml remains available as an optional alternative. It is not
# required for any check. If you use it, point DATABASE_URL at that container.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://orbit_dev:orbit_local_dev@localhost:5432/orbit_dev

# Database used by `pnpm test:db`. It is TRUNCATED between tests, so it must
# never point at a database holding data you care about. The integration
# harness requires this variable, refuses to fall back to DATABASE_URL, and
# aborts unless the connected database is literally named `orbit_test`.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://orbit_dev:orbit_local_dev@localhost:5432/orbit_test

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application ports
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
WEB_PORT=3000
DEMO_PORTAL_PORT=3001
API_PORT=3002
API_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Where the Watchtower dev server proxies /v1. Set this when the API is not on
# this machine, or is on a different port than API_PORT above.
# The library portal's port is not configurable; it is fixed at 3020.
# Ports 3010 and 3102 are RESERVED for the end-to-end test stack and are
# refused to every application, so a test run never collides with `pnpm dev`.
# ORBIT_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3002

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local artifact storage (implemented in Task 5). Gitignored.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARTIFACT_STORAGE_DIR=./data/artifacts

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model provider (Phase 2.13, ADR-034)
#
# ONE choice, for every model call Orbit makes: drafting an SOP from free text,
# judging a decision at run time, and advising while a person records a
# workflow. All three resolve through @orbit/model-provider, so these variables
# move all of them or none of them.
#
# Two separate questions, deliberately not one variable:
#
#   LLM_PROVIDER    which model family    anthropic (default) | gemini
#   LLM_INVOCATION  how it is reached     direct (default)    | bedrock
#
# That split is what lets the same build run directly against an API key on a
# laptop and through Bedrock once deployed, with no code change. The deployment
# environment decides.
#
# Optional throughout: with nothing configured the API still starts and every
# other route works. POST /v1/sop-drafts reports that generation is unavailable
# and names the missing variable, no judge is wired (a judged agent halts with
# DECISION_JUDGE_UNAVAILABLE), and the recorder simply offers no suggestions.
#
# Two things DO stop the process, on purpose: an unrecognised LLM_PROVIDER or
# LLM_INVOCATION, and the combination LLM_PROVIDER=gemini with
# LLM_INVOCATION=bedrock. Bedrock does not serve Gemini, so that configuration
# cannot be satisfied by anything, and startup is where an impossible
# environment should be reported rather than the first request of the day.
# LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic
# LLM_INVOCATION=direct

# --- family: anthropic ---------------------------------------------------
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is a real credential. Keep it in .env, which is gitignored,
# and never commit it or paste it into a document, an SOP, or an issue.
#
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL defaults to the cheapest current Claude model,
# claude-haiku-4-5 (anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5 when invoked through Bedrock).
# Every Orbit call site is bounded structured extraction behind a strict schema
# — the validator is what makes the output trustworthy, not model size — so a
# larger model is a purchase rather than a requirement.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5

# --- family: gemini ------------------------------------------------------
# GEMINI_API_KEY is a real credential, on the same terms. GOOGLE_API_KEY is
# also read, because the underlying Google client reads it from the environment
# itself — ignoring it would let a key that works report itself as missing.
#
# GEMINI_MODEL defaults to gemini-2.5-flash-lite: Google's cheapest generally
# available model that still supports the function calling structured output is
# built on. Model availability changes faster than this file does.
#
# NOT TESTED AGAINST REAL GOOGLE. There are no Google credentials in this
# repository's environment. The Gemini path is structurally correct — same
# selection, same client contract, same token-usage shape, same budgets, priced
# in the rate table, covered by tests that construct a client and never call one
# — and is not demonstrated end to end. Treat the first real call as the test.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash-lite

# --- invocation: bedrock -------------------------------------------------
# LLM_INVOCATION=bedrock reaches Claude models through ChatBedrockConverse.
#
# Orbit holds NO AWS credentials of its own and offers no variable for one.
# They resolve through the AWS SDK's default credential provider chain —
# environment variables, a shared profile, SSO, an instance role, IRSA — which
# is what everything else in an AWS account already uses.
#
# The only thing Orbit must be told is the region, because Bedrock is
# region-scoped. AWS_REGION is read if set; ORBIT_BEDROCK_REGION overrides it
# for the case where Orbit must call a different region than the rest of the
# process. With neither set, the model-backed features report themselves
# unavailable and say so.
# ORBIT_BEDROCK_REGION=us-east-1
#
# A cross-region inference profile needs its geography prefix in the model id
# and its own entry in ORBIT_LLM_RATES_USD_PER_MTOK, since the id is the key:
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=us.anthropic.claude-haiku-4-5
#
# NOT TESTED AGAINST REAL AWS, on the same terms as Gemini above.

# --- superseded names ----------------------------------------------------
# ORBIT_LLM_PROVIDER and ORBIT_LLM_MODEL still work and mean what they always
# meant: ORBIT_LLM_PROVIDER=bedrock sets LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic with
# LLM_INVOCATION=bedrock. They log a deprecation notice once at startup, and
# the new names win when both are set. Prefer the new names.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model spend limits (Phase 2.8, ADR-029)
#
# Total tokens — input plus output — allowed in each scope. Every scope is
# checked BEFORE a model call is made, and the most restrictive one that would
# be exceeded is the one reported. Spend is summed from the `model_usage`
# ledger, which holds one row per provider call.
#
# At drafting time neither an agent nor a run exists yet, so the scopes map
# onto what is real then:
#   GLOBAL     — every call in this deployment.
#   PER_AGENT  — every call for one SOP document. A document is 1:1 with the
#                agent it will become, so before publication the two are the
#                same thing.
#   PER_RUN    — one Generate request: the initial call plus its one repair.
#
# All three default to the values below when unset. Removing a ceiling is
# spelled "unlimited", deliberately — a blank or a zero is something you type
# by accident. Anything else unparseable stops the API from starting rather
# than falling back, so a mistyped ceiling is never silently ignored.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORBIT_LLM_TOKEN_BUDGET_GLOBAL=5000000
# ORBIT_LLM_TOKEN_BUDGET_PER_AGENT=500000
# ORBIT_LLM_TOKEN_BUDGET_PER_RUN=100000

# Per-model rates for the cost ESTIMATE, in USD per million tokens, as
# `model=input:output` pairs. Nothing fetches a price list — this figure is an
# estimate computed from these numbers and is labelled as one everywhere it is
# shown. Models not listed here or in the built-in defaults are costed at a
# conservative fallback rather than at zero.
# Bedrock reports its own model ids, so those are separate rows: the built-in
# defaults cover `anthropic.claude-*` at the first-party rates, which is an
# approximation — Bedrock is partner-operated and prices separately.
# Gemini ids are separate rows too, and the built-in defaults cover
# gemini-2.5-flash-lite, gemini-2.5-flash and gemini-2.5-pro at Google's
# published list prices for standard-context prompts.
# ORBIT_LLM_RATES_USD_PER_MTOK=claude-haiku-4-5=1:5,gemini-2.5-flash-lite=0.1:0.4

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Judged decisions at run time (Phase 2.9, ADR-032)
#
# A judged decision returns an index into a closed list of branches the workflow
# already declares. It cannot invent a branch, and an agent only reaches one if
# its published version declares `permissions.model`. With no provider
# configured above, no judge is wired at all and such an agent halts with
# DECISION_JUDGE_UNAVAILABLE rather than running unjudged.
#
# These are separate from the drafting settings because a decision runs on every
# execution and drafting runs once, so the two want different cost profiles.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The model a judged decision uses. Overrides the family's model variable
# (ANTHROPIC_MODEL / GEMINI_MODEL) for this call site only; unset, it falls back
# to that variable and then to the family default.
# ORBIT_LLM_DECISION_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5

# The confidence an answer must clear when a step declares no threshold of its
# own. A number from 0 to 1; default 0.8. A missing confidence fails closed.
# Anything unparseable, or outside 0-1, stops the process rather than quietly
# lowering the bar on every decision in the deployment.
# ORBIT_LLM_DECISION_CONFIDENCE_MIN=0.8

# Execution-time token ceilings for judged decisions. These are the run-time
# counterparts of ORBIT_LLM_TOKEN_BUDGET_PER_AGENT / _PER_RUN above, which
# govern drafting; both pairs draw on the same ORBIT_LLM_TOKEN_BUDGET_GLOBAL
# pot. Same rules: default to set, and "unlimited" is the only way to remove a
# ceiling.
#   PER_AGENT_RUNTIME  every judged decision for one agent, across its versions
#   PER_RUN_EXECUTION  every judged decision within one run
# ORBIT_LLM_TOKEN_BUDGET_PER_AGENT_RUNTIME=1000000
# ORBIT_LLM_TOKEN_BUDGET_PER_RUN_EXECUTION=50000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Browser windows
#
# Two different browsers, two variables, opposite defaults — because they are
# used for opposite things.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The AUTOMATION browser, the one executing an agent. Headless by default,
# because nobody is watching a run. Set to `true` to watch one happen; useful
# for diagnosing a step that behaves differently than it did when recorded.
# `pnpm agent:run --headed` does the same thing for a single CLI run.
# ORBIT_BROWSER_HEADED=true

# The RECORDING browser, the one a person drives. HEADED by default, because a
# recording is somebody doing the task and there is nothing to record if they
# cannot see the page. Set to `true` only for automated tests that drive it
# programmatically.
#
# It opens on the machine running the API, not on the machine running your
# browser: pointed at a remote API from a laptop, no window appears locally.
# ORBIT_RECORDER_HEADLESS=false

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Actor identity
#
# There is no authentication, no user table and no session anywhere in this
# build. This is the whole of Orbit's notion of who did something, and it is
# recorded on runs started from the browser-worker CLI. Defaults to `dev-user`.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORBIT_ACTOR_ID=dev-user
